When a wallet‑popup that asks for permission (e.g., eth_requestAccounts) becomes flaky, it’s usually not a “mystery” bug but the result of one or more of these common factors:

Browser or wallet extension updates – New versions of Chrome, Firefox, or the wallet (MetaMask, Coinbase Wallet, etc.) may change how pop‑ups are throttled, focused, or sandboxed. A recent update can silently break older code that relied on timing or focus assumptions.

Asynchronous race conditions – If you fire multiple eth_* requests without awaiting the previous one, the wallet can queue several pop‑ups or ignore later ones. The same applies when you trigger a request while another UI element (e.g., a modal) is still open.

Improper error handling – Not catching rejection (userRejectedRequest, requestPending, etc.) can leave the UI in an inconsistent state, making the next popup appear “stuck”. Always handle the promise rejection and reset any UI flags.

Popup‑blocking extensions or settings – Some privacy or ad‑blocking extensions now treat wallet pop‑ups as generic pop‑ups and block them unless explicitly allowed.

Chain or network changes – Switching the chainId after a connection but before the permission request can cause the wallet to reject the request silently.

Deprecated APIs – Using older methods (e.g., ethereum.enable()) instead of the current eth_requestAccounts may lead to fallback behaviours that are less reliable.

State management glitches – If your UI state (e.g., a “loading” flag) isn’t cleared after a failed request, the component may think the permission is already granted and suppress the next pop‑up.

Quick diagnostic checklist

✅ Check	How to verify
Browser/extension version	Open the wallet’s extension page, note the version, and compare with the changelog for recent breaking changes.
Single‑request flow	Ensure you only call eth_requestAccounts (or similar) once per user interaction, and await its promise before any other wallet call.
Error handling	Wrap each request in try/catch; on rejection, reset UI flags and optionally show a retry button.
Popup blocker	Disable any ad‑blocker or privacy extensions temporarily; see if the pop‑up appears consistently.
Network change	Verify the chainId matches the wallet’s selected network before sending the request.
Console logs	Look for warnings like “request pending” or “user rejected” in the browser console.
State reset	After any failure, clear relevant state (e.g., isConnecting = false).

Typical fix pattern

js
async function connectWallet() {
  try {
    // Reset UI flags first
    setConnecting(true);
    // Single request – await before any other eth_* call
    const accounts = await window.ethereum.request({ method: 'eth_requestAccounts' });
    // Use the account, update UI
    setAccount(accounts[0]);
  } catch (err) {
    console.warn('Wallet connection failed:', err);
    // Reset UI so the next click shows a fresh popup
    setConnecting(false);
    // Optionally inform the user
    alert('Please approve the connection request in your wallet.');
  }
}

If after these checks the issue persists, try reproducing it in a clean browser profile (no extensions) and, if it still appears, check the wallet’s release notes or open a support ticket with the wallet provider.

